      "obligation": "refuter:5:1",
      "id": "def-frobenius-matrix-norm",
      "location": "Remarks",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Remark says, “The Frobenius norm is not an induced matrix norm” and “is not induced by any vector p-norm on R^n for n≥2.” For a real 1-by-2 matrix A=(a b), however, ||A||_F=sqrt(a^2+b^2), while its induced vector 2-norm is sup_{||x||_2≤1}|ax_0+bx_1|=sqrt(a^2+b^2), by Cauchy–Schwarz with equality at x proportional to (a,b).",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "e56eb4ac81df5701de9efdc1259f01c5024dc811e88c5f47791f3648c8fb96db"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:2",
      "id": "def-induced-matrix-p-norm",
      "location": "Definition",
      "defect": "ill-formed",
      "evidence": "The Definition restricts its parameter to “p∈Q with p≥1,” and its final Remark says it “inherits that restriction verbatim.” Nevertheless the Definition asserts that the explicit values “at p=1 and p=∞” are supplied later. The cited vector-norm definition confirms that ||·||_∞ is separately defined and that “the subscript ∞ is a name, not a number”; no induced matrix infinity norm is defined by this rational-p definition.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "86cab17d1f8079291c2f84e6c03ef3928c2e53990c968665233040088f32aedf"
    },
    {
